Yes. I went looking specifically for the thing everyone has to walk through even when we don’t know who wins.

The clearest tollbooth I see: ASML

ASML Holding is unusually close to the literal form of the pattern.

Think about the AI-chip war:

NVDA \leftrightarrow AMD \leftrightarrow custom\ ASICs \leftrightarrow Intel \leftrightarrow future\ architectures

We don’t know exactly who wins.

But sufficiently advanced chips eventually encounter:

\boxed{\text{Lithography}}

and at the cutting edge:

\boxed{\text{EUV}\rightarrow ASML}

Reuters reports ASML has about 94% of the overall lithography market and effectively a monopoly in cutting-edge EUV, with no serious commercial rival. TSMC, Samsung, SK Hynix and Intel are all moving toward ASML’s next-generation High-NA machines, which cost roughly $400M each. 

Even better for our thesis, ASML is reportedly nearly fully booked for EUV through 2027 and is investigating production above 110 EUV systems in 2028 because AI-related demand is so strong. 

That’s the tollbooth.

Why I like the structure

If Nvidia wins:

NVDA\rightarrow foundry\rightarrow ASML

If AMD wins:

AMD\rightarrow foundry\rightarrow ASML

If hyperscalers increasingly design their own accelerators:

Google/Meta/Amazon/OpenAI\ ASIC
\rightarrow foundry
\rightarrow ASML

If memory becomes the bottleneck:

HBM/DRAM\ advancement
\rightarrow SK\ Hynix/Samsung/Micron
\rightarrow ASML.

That’s exactly why TSMC and ASML just formed an initiative around larger photomasks for High-NA EUV, while Intel says it has already processed more than one million High-NA wafers. 

So ASML doesn’t need to predict the winning chip.

It needs advanced semiconductor complexity to continue.

