There are actually several layers of tollbooths.

Physical tollbooth — ASML. You want leading-edge manufacturing capability? You need its lithography equipment.

Design tollbooths — Cadence Design Systems and Synopsys. Whoever designs the chip still needs sophisticated EDA/IP. Cadence just reported core EDA revenue +18%, IP revenue >40%, record $8.1B backlog and raised its 2026 outlook. Synopsys reported Q3 revenue of $2.477B versus $1.740B a year earlier and raised full-year expectations on AI-driven demand. 

Networking tollbooth — potentially Nvidia/Broadcom/Arista. Every accelerator eventually has to communicate. IDC says the datacenter Ethernet-switch market is exploding; Nvidia reached 21.5% share in Q1 while Arista had 20.7%, so unlike ASML this is a contested tollbooth, not a monopoly. 

Platform tollbooth — Nvidia’s emerging NVLink strategy. This one is especially clever. d-Matrix is building a competing inference processor but adopting Nvidia’s NVLink Fusion, MGX racks, networking, CPUs and other infrastructure to deploy it. 

That means Nvidia is attempting:

\text{If you beat my chip, pay my bridge.}

That may ultimately be more valuable than defending 100% accelerator share.

Goblin ranking right now

For purity of the Tollbooth pattern, I’d rank them:

\boxed{ASML > CDNS/SNPS > NVDA\ platform > networking}

But best business ≠ best stock at today’s price. That’s our next assay.

I want to put ASML through the full Goblin grinder now: valuation, 5/20/60/252-day behavior, historical drawdowns, earnings sensitivity, China/export-control risk, High-NA adoption scenarios, free cash flow, plausible 3–5 year returns, and then compare it directly against CDNS, SNPS, AVGO and ANET.

That should tell us whether we’ve merely found the tollbooth—or whether we’ve found the tollbooth at a price worth paying.